WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.871
- Super excited to be here.
This is my tenth year

00:00:01.871 --> 00:00:06.667
at South-By, which is
insane and I'm glad to go

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.875
back to the form, at two
years ago or three years ago

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.959
I decided to do a full Q&amp;amp;A,
and it was a lot of fun

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:14.125
and really set the
tone for what I did

00:00:14.125 --> 00:00:16.500
with my last book and
things of that nature.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.208
There's a mic right up here,
I'm gonna probably talk

00:00:19.208 --> 00:00:21.875
for only two or three or
four minutes, so if you've ever

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:24.125
wanted to ask a question,
I think it's a really good time

00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:25.959
to actually get up
from your seat right now

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:29.291
and start getting
into line, so do that.

00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:34.166
As you're debating
your questions, let me talk

00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:35.959
to you about what's on my mind.

00:00:35.959 --> 00:00:37.250
I know there's a
good mix of people here

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.333
who know quite a bit
about me, and I'm sure there's

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:41.792
a good mix of people who don't.

00:00:41.792 --> 00:00:43.917
For my whole life I called
myself a businessman.

00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:46.041
We now call
that an entrepreneur.

00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.750
I've always been selling stuff,
and what it synthesized to

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.875
for the people that are
paying attention to me closest

00:00:51.875 --> 00:00:55.542
is I'm really,
really focused on attention.

00:00:55.542 --> 00:00:57.750
All the happiness that
I've had professionally

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.834
has been my ability to
understand where the consumer

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:04.208
attention is while the
world underestimates it.

00:01:04.208 --> 00:01:08.291
This conference, the
people in this room, are

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.000
easily aware of
where things are going.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.375
You wouldn't be
at this conference.

00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:15.041
You wouldn't be
coming to these kind of talks

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.250
if you didn't understand where

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.125
the attention is
either or about to be.

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:22.125
I think this space,
our space, tends to think

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:24.708
attention is gonna be in
places a little bit too quickly.

00:01:24.708 --> 00:01:26.417
For example, the
last two or three years

00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:28.750
I've been debating
very aggressively with a lot

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.959
of my Silicon Valley
friends that VR

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:34.000
on the consumer level is much
further away than we think.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.333
The technology's there, but it

00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:37.500
kinda reminds
me of internet '92.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.250
It's gonna take time
for normal people to,

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.583
how many people,
by show of hands,

00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:42.375
outside of people
that are in the business,

00:01:42.375 --> 00:01:45.542
know of a human
being who actually spends

00:01:45.542 --> 00:01:49.250
two hours a day,
or an hour a day, in VR?

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.667
A human being
not in the business?

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:55.458
Three, so we got
a long way to go.

00:01:55.458 --> 00:01:58.041
And I think that's the things
we need to think about, right?

00:01:58.041 --> 00:02:02.291
But to me, I'm unemotional,
like Twitter,

00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:04.959
email marketing,
YouTube, these are the platforms

00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:07.208
that through the
years really helped me grow

00:02:07.208 --> 00:02:10.458
my wine business,
my brand, my agency,

00:02:10.458 --> 00:02:12.041
but the truth is I'm unemotional

00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:14.125
about whether
they're here tomorrow.

00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:16.125
I'm sad that
Twitter's ad product

00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:17.583
isn't as good as
I'd like it to be,

00:02:17.583 --> 00:02:19.166
but I would
never waste my money,

00:02:19.166 --> 00:02:21.625
tell you to waste your
money, or guide my startups

00:02:21.625 --> 00:02:24.083
or companies to waste
their money in that platform.

00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:27.959
To me, I'm obsessed with
the underpriced nature

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.708
of Facebook's ads
Marketplace right now.

00:02:30.708 --> 00:02:33.667
But the day it becomes
overpriced, I won't cry.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:36.125
I'll just move on to
wherever our attention,

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:39.625
there's an unemotional attention
grab that I'm focused on.

00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:42.291
Then I think a lot of the
other things that may happen

00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:46.041
here is
I'm pretty undereducated,

00:02:46.041 --> 00:02:47.250
in hindsight, right?

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.208
I was an
extraordinarily poor student.

00:02:50.208 --> 00:02:53.583
And as I continue
to hear things, like,

00:02:53.583 --> 00:02:54.917
there's some amazing
thing I saw on social,

00:02:54.917 --> 00:02:58.750
my feed the other day,
a graffiti artist saying,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.792
the creative people that
are winning, are the kids

00:03:01.792 --> 00:03:05.291
that got through adulthood
without being tainted, right?

00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:07.500
And I actually
think that so much

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.792
of what I think about
just comes from a pure audit.

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:12.583
I don't have a
preconceived notion

00:03:12.583 --> 00:03:15.917
when I look at most things,
and I think that blindness.

00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:17.208
When I went into
the agency world,

00:03:17.208 --> 00:03:19.166
right now I'm
running an 800 person agency.

00:03:19.166 --> 00:03:22.917
We've gone from zero to
$130 million in revenue.

00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:25.000
When I started it,
people made fun of me, and said,

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.583
just 'cause I had a
lot of Twitter followers,

00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:29.083
how was I gonna compete
with real Madison Avenue.

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:31.750
For me, the reason we've
been so grossly successful

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.375
is I didn't have any of the
baggage of the agency world.

00:03:34.375 --> 00:03:36.417
I knew nothing about the
agency world in the same way

00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:38.125
I didn't know anything
about the wine business

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:39.542
when I went into
my dad's business.

00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:42.542
And whatever I operate for
the next two or three things

00:03:42.542 --> 00:03:45.625
until I buy the
Jets and win Super Bowls,

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:49.625
I'm gonna be very blind to it.

00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:52.125
I implore people to
make things more simple.

00:03:52.125 --> 00:03:53.792
I think things
are extremely simple

00:03:53.792 --> 00:03:55.875
if you allow
them to be that way.

00:03:55.875 --> 00:03:58.667
I think we make
them very complicated,

00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:00.083
and I think a
lot of people here,

00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:02.125
how many people
are running or work

00:04:02.125 --> 00:04:03.875
at a startup, 
early stage startup?

00:04:03.875 --> 00:04:06.417
Raise your hands high,
I just wanna get a sense.

00:04:06.417 --> 00:04:10.250
A lot, so to me one of the
things that's very devastating

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.375
and the difference of being
here today versus 10 years ago

00:04:13.375 --> 00:04:15.750
is the people that
were here 10 years ago

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.041
were building products and
were trying to build products

00:04:19.041 --> 00:04:21.291
and, or businesses,
one or the other.

00:04:21.291 --> 00:04:24.458
The far majority of
startups in our ecosystem today

00:04:24.458 --> 00:04:27.583
are building
financial arbitrage machines.

00:04:27.583 --> 00:04:29.834
All they're doing is
trying to hit certain metrics

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:32.542
to raise the next round
of capital to keep going

00:04:32.542 --> 00:04:35.083
until some sort
of imaginary exit.

00:04:35.083 --> 00:04:37.625
And that's scary to me
because for anybody else here

00:04:37.625 --> 00:04:39.041
who's following
the business world,

00:04:39.041 --> 00:04:40.041
we've now lived through eight or

00:04:40.041 --> 00:04:43.083
nine very good
years in our economy.

00:04:43.083 --> 00:04:44.708
That plays
itself out eventually.

00:04:44.708 --> 00:04:46.125
And when there is no more money

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:48.000
and you actually
have to run a business,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.625
all those things you
cared about, like CAC and LTV

00:04:50.625 --> 00:04:53.041
and all these other
lovely, important things

00:04:53.041 --> 00:04:54.959
become far less important than

00:04:54.959 --> 00:04:57.208
do you make more money
than you lose each month?

00:04:57.208 --> 00:04:59.083
Let me just remind
everybody, you're not a

00:04:59.083 --> 00:05:02.792
superstar entrepreneur when
you're losing money each month.

00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:04.375
(audience laughter)

00:05:04.375 --> 00:05:06.041
There's a lot of
interesting themes

00:05:06.041 --> 00:05:08.250
that are in play
right now, and so

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.375
those are the things that
I like to pay attention to.

00:05:10.375 --> 00:05:13.500
I reverse engineer
human behavior, and I deploy

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.750
my actions into places where
I think there's opportunity.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.500
That's my thesis,
that's what I roll on,

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.834
and most of it's
very tried and true.

00:05:21.834 --> 00:05:24.875
I love HR, I love people.

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:27.041
I love people's behavior,

00:05:27.041 --> 00:05:28.834
and I love consumer trends.

00:05:28.834 --> 00:05:31.250
This is the place
to be for that stuff.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.708
Thanks for having me
and I'm ready

00:05:32.708 --> 00:05:35.166
to answer questions,
whether very tactical of, like,

00:05:35.166 --> 00:05:38.333
how to run a Snapchat
filter ad or philosophical

00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:40.083
of, like, how do you tell your

00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:42.333
mom that she's
ruining your life?

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:45.083
(audience laughter)

00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:47.667
Cool, name and question.

00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:50.417
- Steven Rosenberg.
My question is, so I watch you

00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:53.000
go all over the country
and all over the world

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.458
and people ask
you the same questions--

00:05:54.458 --> 00:05:54.875
- Yes.

00:05:54.875 --> 00:05:56.000
- [Steven] over and
over and over,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.625
I wanna add value and ask you,

00:05:57.625 --> 00:06:00.166
what kind of questions
do you want people to ask?

00:06:00.166 --> 00:06:03.375
You don't have to answer the
same question over and over.

00:06:03.375 --> 00:06:06.458
- So Steve, this is literally
the question I hate the most.

00:06:06.458 --> 00:06:08.417
(audience laughter)

00:06:08.417 --> 00:06:09.417
- [Steven] Okay, really?

00:06:09.417 --> 00:06:11.500
- I'm not even kidding.
- [Steven] Really?

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.041
- I'll explain in a minute.
- [Steven] Okay, yeah.

00:06:13.041 --> 00:06:14.458
- Literally,
I would've preferred

00:06:14.458 --> 00:06:16.542
you ask me the
same cliche question.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.542
- [Steven]Really?
- Yes, I'll tell you why.

00:06:18.542 --> 00:06:21.166
- When you're answering
people's questions,

00:06:21.166 --> 00:06:23.792
you need to provide them
value, not yourself value.

00:06:23.792 --> 00:06:24.625
- [Steven] Correct.

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:26.583
- Do you know why 98%
of the South-By keynotes

00:06:26.583 --> 00:06:28.333
this year are gonna suck shit?

00:06:28.333 --> 00:06:29.291
- [Steven] Because they're not--

00:06:29.291 --> 00:06:32.333
- No, because they're
doing press releases for their

00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:33.166
- [Steve] Right.
- best behavior,

00:06:33.166 --> 00:06:34.083
not for the audiences.

00:06:34.083 --> 00:06:35.625
The reason I'm doing a keynote,

00:06:35.625 --> 00:06:38.667
a Q&amp;amp;A session is I
wanna give you something.

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:40.625
I'm plenty
comfortable self-promoting

00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:42.166
plenty of other times,

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:44.417
and so I don't have
any question.

00:06:44.417 --> 00:06:46.917
Listen, there's nothing
I want to be asked.

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:49.083
All I want to do is give
people what their question is.

00:06:49.083 --> 00:06:51.000
I don't mind answering
the same question

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.166
over and over, you know why?

00:06:52.166 --> 00:06:55.959
We have seven to 11 basic
themes that are fucking us up.

00:06:55.959 --> 00:06:58.375
- [Steven] Right, so,
alright let me ask you this.

00:06:58.375 --> 00:06:59.291
- Fine.
- [Steven] What do you think

00:06:59.291 --> 00:07:02.000
of eco-capitalism
moving forward in 2017?

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.083
- I'm sorry one more time?

00:07:03.083 --> 00:07:04.583
- [Steven] Eco-capitalism.

00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:06.208
- Like, break it down for me.

00:07:06.208 --> 00:07:08.583
Another great thing about Q&amp;amp;A
is I try not to answer shit

00:07:08.583 --> 00:07:10.375
I don't understand
- [Steven] Yeah so basically,

00:07:10.375 --> 00:07:11.959
(audience laughter)
corporations being able

00:07:11.959 --> 00:07:14.166
to profit from
their waste stream,

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:16.875
though its really wasteful,
and you have to pay for waste.

00:07:16.875 --> 00:07:18.458
And they're using
social to change that.

00:07:18.458 --> 00:07:21.250
- So, in that vein,
I don't know all the details,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.625
but I'll go
thematically with you.

00:07:22.625 --> 00:07:24.625
- [Steven] Please.
- Companies are very basic.

00:07:24.625 --> 00:07:25.291
- [Steven] Okay.

00:07:25.291 --> 00:07:27.458
- They never do good things

00:07:27.458 --> 00:07:28.834
unless it's
good for the company.

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:29.667
- [Steven] Right.

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:32.000
- So, if there's a
way to incentivize,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.834
it's like when people
are CEO's and they're mad

00:07:33.834 --> 00:07:36.041
at their sales team
for selling the wrong shit,

00:07:36.041 --> 00:07:39.291
but meanwhile the CEO's bonusing
them on that shit behavior,

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:40.458
I'm like, you're a shithead.

00:07:40.458 --> 00:07:42.667
Change the
bonuses and they'll adapt.

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.834
You wanna fix the environment?

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:47.667
Make and incentivize
every company to do that,

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:50.333
they will do it
every single time.

00:07:50.333 --> 00:07:52.458
- [Steven] Correct,
but it's hard for them

00:07:52.458 --> 00:07:53.959
to get on board initially,

00:07:53.959 --> 00:07:57.250
even though it
capitalistically makes sense,

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.708
for them to listen to
that, because they hear

00:07:58.708 --> 00:08:00.792
environment and they go oh.

00:08:00.792 --> 00:08:03.000
- Yeah, I would tell you
that I don't believe that,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.458
and let me explain why.

00:08:04.458 --> 00:08:06.542
I'm not sure who's
feeding them that information,

00:08:06.542 --> 00:08:10.667
but I promise you every CFO
of every Fortune 5,000 company

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.250
in the world, unless they decide

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.542
to make it a political issue,
which I'm very empathetic to,

00:08:15.542 --> 00:08:18.417
is going to always make
the dollar and cent decision.

00:08:18.417 --> 00:08:19.708
- [Steven] Right, I mean doesn't

00:08:19.708 --> 00:08:22.542
VaynerMedia
spend money on waste?

00:08:22.542 --> 00:08:24.708
- Of course.
- [Steven] A lot.

00:08:24.708 --> 00:08:26.041
- I don't know, to be frank.

00:08:26.041 --> 00:08:28.000
- [Steven] I'm sure,
yeah, okay, thank you.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.417
- You're welcome.

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:33.291
Me?

00:08:33.291 --> 00:08:34.332
Seriously?

00:08:34.332 --> 00:08:35.707
(audience laughter)

00:08:35.707 --> 00:08:36.833
That is literally the first time

00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:38.542
I've ever fucking
heard that in my life.

00:08:38.542 --> 00:08:39.707
Can you guys pump me up?

00:08:39.707 --> 00:08:41.041
Got it, I'll go harder.

00:08:41.041 --> 00:08:43.125
(audience laughter)

00:08:43.125 --> 00:08:44.125
What's up my man?

00:08:44.125 --> 00:08:45.458
- [Trevor] How are you Gary?

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:46.542
- What's your name?

00:08:46.542 --> 00:08:48.291
- [Trevor] Trevor Williams, 
Austin, Texas.

00:08:48.291 --> 00:08:49.125
- Love it.

00:08:49.125 --> 00:08:50.333
- [Trevor] First off,
real quick, huge fan.

00:08:50.333 --> 00:08:51.166
- Thank you.

00:08:51.166 --> 00:08:52.083
- [Trevor] I'm limiting
myself on my goals,

00:08:52.083 --> 00:08:54.083
and you kinda helped
me blast through those.

00:08:54.083 --> 00:08:54.917
- Thank you.

00:08:54.917 --> 00:08:56.542
- [Trevor] We have a
local company, IoVitamine,

00:08:56.542 --> 00:08:59.250
we do IV hydration therapy,
great for hangovers by the way.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.583
South-By a good time here.
- So the kind of shit where

00:09:00.583 --> 00:09:02.041
you get drunk and
you go into a place

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:02.875
and they put an IV in you?

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:03.708
- [Trevor] Exactly.
- Okay.

00:09:03.708 --> 00:09:05.166
- [Trevor] It's not just that
though, it's overall health,

00:09:05.166 --> 00:09:07.500
fatigue, it's overall wellness.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:08.333
- Okay.

00:09:08.333 --> 00:09:09.917
- [Trevor] Great response,
new category,

00:09:09.917 --> 00:09:11.250
the only one here in Austin.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.083
There's only about a handful

00:09:12.083 --> 00:09:13.458
of them across
the United States.

00:09:13.458 --> 00:09:15.917
We're trying to raise awareness
of how it makes you feel,

00:09:15.917 --> 00:09:18.583
how it helps you,
and we're overwhelmed.

00:09:18.583 --> 00:09:20.500
We have four partners
that are working full-time,

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:21.750
we're trying to
scale it pretty quickly,

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.291
so we're doing all of the
standing operating procedures.

00:09:24.291 --> 00:09:26.083
So how do we--
- You're trying to scale

00:09:26.083 --> 00:09:27.208
quickly because
you feel like you

00:09:27.208 --> 00:09:30.041
could be commoditized if
people open up other shops?

00:09:30.041 --> 00:09:31.291
- [Trevor] Yeah, we
just want to be first.

00:09:31.291 --> 00:09:33.792
It's a first to market play,
it's not really controllable.

00:09:33.792 --> 00:09:35.291
- Not always, right?

00:09:35.291 --> 00:09:37.667
It's first to market
play a lot of times on brand,

00:09:37.667 --> 00:09:39.625
but not on locations, and
this is where a lot of people

00:09:39.625 --> 00:09:42.125
get fucked up, they
overexpand and they lose,

00:09:42.125 --> 00:09:44.542
Krispy Kreme, things of
